<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">McCarthyism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1940s–1950s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Second Red Scare</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">McCarthyism was the practice of accusing and investigating left-wing Americans for communist and Soviet influence on American institutions and for Soviet espionage, from the late 1940s through the 1950s. It began before Senator Joseph McCarthy's fame, with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a>'s 1947 order screening federal employees for loyalty, and its best-known forms were McCarthy's own investigations and the hearings of the House Un-American Activities Committee. Many lives were ruined by blacklists, dismissals and verdicts later overturned. In 1954 the Senate voted 67 to 22 to condemn McCarthy, and rulings of the Supreme Court under Earl Warren helped bring the Second Red Scare to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mccarthyism.jpg" alt="McCarthyism" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Senate · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Welch-McCarthy-Hearings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Anti-communist measures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> predated Senator Joseph McCarthy. The Smith Act of 1940 made it an offence to advocate overthrowing the government by force. The House Committee on Un-American Activities, formed in 1938 and first known as the Dies Committee, turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> early, and its investigation of the espionage charges against Alger Hiss in 1948 convinced many of the usefulness of congressional committees. Truman's Executive Order 9835 of 21 March 1947 required loyalty screening of federal employees, probably in part a response to the Republican sweep of the 1946 elections.</p><p>McCarthy's own part began publicly on 9 February 1950, when he told a Republican women's club in Wheeling, West Virginia, that he held a list of known communists working in the State Department. The speech brought a flood of press attention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Loyalty review boards and committees operated in federal, state and local governments and in private agencies. Between 1949 and 1954 Congress carried out 109 investigations through HUAC, the Senate Internal Security Subcommittee and the Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> took office in 1953 he strengthened the loyalty programme and narrowed the avenues of appeal. In 1954 J. Robert Oppenheimer lost his security clearance after a four-week hearing.</p><p>On 25 November 1947 the heads of the major film studios issued the Waldorf Statement, announcing the dismissal of the Hollywood Ten and marking the start of the Hollywood blacklist, which employers never publicly admitted. By 1952 several states had banned communists from public employment and required loyalty oaths. The number imprisoned is in the hundreds, and some ten or twelve thousand lost their jobs. Suspected homosexuality was also a common ground for targeting, and over 5,000 federal workers were fired in what many call the lavender scare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Support came from the American Legion, militantly anti-communist women's groups and a wider coalition opposed to internationalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> welfare programmes. Critics included Truman, who in vetoing the McCarran Internal Security Act of 1950 wrote that in a free country men are punished for crimes, never for opinions. On 2 December 1954 the Senate voted 67 to 22 to condemn McCarthy. John Henry Faulk, dismissed by CBS Radio after being marked unfit by the private firm AWARE, won his suit against it in 1962.</p><p>Under Chief Justice Earl Warren the Supreme Court issued rulings that helped end the era. Declassified Soviet archives and the Venona decryptions later showed substantial Soviet espionage in the 1940s and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> funded and influenced the Communist Party USA. Some historians, including Ellen Schrecker, hold that Hooverism, after FBI head J. Edgar Hoover, describes the phenomenon better than McCarthyism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “McCarthyism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward Shils's article of 1954 on <b>McCarthyism</b> and Hélio Jaguaribe's text of the same year on Brazil opened its wider use, and a conference at the London School of Economics in 1967 failed to produce a shared theory.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/populism">Populism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
